

{"id":"01eeb2b6-9343-4388-847e-4eb1df13c111","object":"chat.completion","created":1786437871,"model":"deepseek-v4-pro","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"record_id\": \"CA12_CORE_011\",\n  \"decisions\": [\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_01\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_01\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 8,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_02\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_02\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 16,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_03\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_03\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_07\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 7,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_06\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_03\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_06\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 22,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_04\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_03\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_04\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 26,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_05\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_01\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_10\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 22,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    }\n  ]\n}","reasoning_content":"We are given a public task with baseline decisions and we need to produce a repair. The assignment order contract is \"FIXED_UAV_ASSIGNMENT_AND_FIXED_UAV_ORDER\", so the UAV assignments and order of missions for each UAV are fixed as in the baseline. Deadhead contract is \"FIXED_REGISTERED_INCOMING_DEADHEAD_PATH\", so incoming deadhead paths are fixed? but missions have \"incoming_deadhead\": null, so no incoming deadhead anyway.\n\nWe must respect fixed UAV assignment and mission order, route compatibility, release/due windows, incoming deadhead, charging, SOC/reserve, corridor/pad/charger capacity, closures, and intervention overrides. Optimize lexicographically: urgent holds, total holds, repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy.\n\nWe must return exactly one JSON with record_id and decisions for each mission. Each decision must have: mission_id, uav_id from the fixed assignment, held (boolean), path_id (if held true, null, else a valid path_id from mission's path_options), depart_slot (if held true, null, else an integer slot when the service flight departs), and charge_blocks (if held true, 0, else an integer from 0 to max_charge_blocks (3)). So for each mission we can choose to hold it or not. Held means it's canceled? \"held\": true means mission is not flown? Probably it means the mission is not served (held on ground). Lexicographic objective: first minimize urgent holds (missions with priority \"urgent\"? all missions here have \"priority\":\"routine\", so none urgent. So urgent holds = 0. Then minimize total holds (number of held missions). Then repair distance? Probably the sum of distance_km for flown missions? But with deadhead? \"repair distance\" might be the total distance flown (service flights + incoming deadhead). Incoming deadhead paths are null, so only service flight distance. Then weighted tardiness: likely lateness beyond due slot weighted by some factor? delay plus charge wait: total delay? then energy: total energy consumed.\n\nBut the task says: \"Optimize the stated task lexicographically: urgent holds, total holds, repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy.\" So we want a feasible solution that minimizes, in order: 1) number of urgent holds (none here, so 0), 2) total holds (we want to fly as many as possible? Or maybe we are penalized for holds? Minimize total holds means we want to avoid holding missions if possible), 3) repair distance (probably total distance flown in km, including possible deadhead, but there is none), 4) weighted tardiness, 5) delay plus charge wait, 6) energy. So we should try to fly all missions without holding if possible. If not possible, we might need to hold some.\n\nNow, check the baseline: it has all missions not held, with specific paths and depart slots. The intervention overrides include initial_soc_overrides: UAV_02 initial_soc changed to 1.781578 instead of 6.810778 (the instance had 6.810778). So a major reduction. Also pad_capacity_overrides: VP_03 slot 7 capacity reduced to 1 (from 2). closed_corridor_slots: COR_10 closed from slot 10 through 49 (almost all horizon, since horizon_slots 47? Actually COR_10 closed 10-49, but horizon is 47, so slots 10-47 are closed. Also COR_07 closed slots 10-21. These closures affect path choices.\n\nWe must check if baselines are still feasible after interventions. If not, we need to find alternative paths and depart slots, possibly charging, to make all missions feasible. We may also hold missions if they cannot be flown within their windows and resource constraints.\n\nLet's list missions with their fixed UAV and order:\n\nMission_01: UAV_02, seq 0. Orig VP_03, dest VP_01. Release 7, due 13.\nPath options for M01 on UAV_02: PATH_01 (energy 0.240826, travel_slots 2, corridor COR_11, max_wind 1), PATH_03 (energy 4.214448, travel_slots 7, corridor COR_08, max_wind 1), PATH_05 (energy 0.226, travel_slots 3, corridor COR_04, max_wind 0). Corridor closures: COR_08, COR_11, COR_04 are not in closed list (only COR_10 and COR_07 closed). So all paths are open.\n\nMission_02: UAV_02, seq 1. Orig VP_01, dest VP_06. Release 16, due 25. Only PATH_02 (energy 0.09633, travel_slots 1, COR_05). COR_05 open.\n\nMission_03: UAV_03, seq 0. Orig VP_03, dest VP_01. Release 7, due 9. Only PATH_07 (energy 0.168856, travel_slots 2, COR_12). COR_12 open.\n\nMission_04: UAV_03, seq 2? Wait, sequence indices: M03 seq 0, M06 seq 1, M04 seq 2? Actually missions for UAV_03: M03 seq0, M06 seq1, M04 seq2. Order: M03 -> M06 -> M04. But in baseline, M03 depart 7 (path_07), M06 depart 10 (path_09? Actually baseline M06 depart 10, path_09 but path_09 is closed later? Let's check), M04 depart 26. So we must maintain this order. M06 is seq1 for UAV_03. M04 is seq2. So after M03, UAV_03 is at VP_01 at slot? M03 travel 2 slots from VP_03 to VP_01 departing slot 7: arrives at slot 7+2 = 9. Due is 9, so on time. Then turnaround of 1 slot at VP_01, so ready to start M06 after slot 10. M06 orig VP_01, dest VP_07. Release 10, due 27. Path options: PATH_06 (COR_07, travel 2, energy 0.154382), PATH_09 (COR_10, travel 2, energy 0.154382), PATH_11 (COR_02, travel 14, energy 1.235059). But closures: COR_10 closed slots 10-47, so if we depart M06 at slot 10 or later, COR_10 is closed at slot 10 (closed 10-49). So PATH_09 using COR_10 is unavailable for any departure slot >=10. COR_07 closed slots 10-21. So PATH_06 using COR_07 is closed for depart slots 10-21. So if we want to depart M06 at slot 10, both PATH_06 and PATH_09 are closed (COR_07 closed 10-21, COR_10 closed 10-47). Only PATH_11 (COR_02) is open, travel 14 slots. Energy 1.235059. That could be possible if we have enough SOC. But UAV_03 starts with SOC 4.536474, after M03? M03 uses PATH_07 energy 0.168856, so after M03 + turnaround + charge? We can charge after M03. Turnaround 1 slot, then we can charge before M06? M06's release is 10, and we need to depart after charging. We could depart later. But if we depart at slot 10 using PATH_06 or PATH_09, they are closed. So we must use PATH_11 with 14 slots. That would arrive at VP_07 at slot 10+14 = 24. Due 27, so ok. But then we have M04: orig VP_07, dest VP_02, release 26, due 30. After M06 arriving VP_07 at 24, turnaround 1 slot at VP_07, then ready slot 25. Release for M04 is 26, so we could depart at 26. M04 only PATH_04 (COR_06, travel 1, energy 1.235059). So total sequence for UAV_03: M03 depart 7, path_07, arrive 9. Turnaround 1 slot (slot 9-10). Then charge? maybe we need charge to have enough for M06 and M04. M06 energy 1.235059, reserve 2.5. So needed for M06: energy for incoming deadhead? No incoming deadhead. So arrival at VP_01 with SOC after M03: start SOC 4.536474 - M03 energy 0.168856 = 4.367618. Turnaround: no consumption during turnaround. Then before M06, we must have enough SOC to cover service flight (1.235059) + reserve (2.5). So minimum SOC at start of M06 must be >= 1.235059+2.5 = 3.735059. Current SOC after M03 is 4.367618, which is >3.735059, so no charge needed for M06. But reserve check is after deducing incoming deadhead + service flight. So after M06 flight, SOC = 4.367618 - 1.235059 = 3.132559. Reserve must be >=2.5, so ok. Then at VP_07, after turnaround (1 slot), for M04: energy 1.235059, reserve 2.5. Arrival SOC after M06: 3.132559. That is less than needed for M04 (1.235059+2.5=3.735059). So we need to charge after M06 and before M04. After M06 arrives VP_07 at slot 24. Turnaround 1 slot occupies slot 24-25? Normally, the order: incoming deadhead -> service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge. So the turnaround occurs immediately after service flight arrival. The post-service charge starts after turnaround. So for M06, after arriving at 24, turnaround would be slot 24-25? Typically, arrival at start of slot? Let's clarify slot timing: depart_slot is the start of the flight, and travel_slots is the number of slots the flight occupies. Arrival at the end of travel_slots? In many models, if depart at slot t, flight uses slots t, t+1,..., t+travel_slots-1? Or arrives at t+travel_slots? The baseline had M03 depart 7, travel 2, so arrival at 9? That suggests arrival slot = depart_slot + travel_slots. So M03 depart 7, travel 2, arrival at 9. Then turnaround of 1 slot: probably the UAV is unavailable during that slot at the destination. So after arrival at 9, turnaround at slot 9-10? Then post-service charge can start at 10. For M06, if depart 10, travel 14, arrival at 24. Turnaround 24-25? Then post-service charge starting at 25. But M04 release is 26, so if we start charge at 25, we can charge blocks until just before M04 departure. Charge block is 1 slot per block? The fleet has charge_kwh_per_block: for UAV_03 it's 0.95 kWh per block. So we need to raise SOC from 3.132559 to at least 3.735059 (needed for M04) = need 0.6025 kWh. Each charge block provides 0.95, so one block is enough. So we could charge 1 block post-M06, at slot 25, and then depart M04 at slot 26. But slot 25 is the turnaround? Actually, the order: after arrival, turnaround occupies the slot? We need to respect the event order: \"incoming deadhead -> service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge\". So the turnaround happens right after service flight, and the UAV is occupied during that slot. Then post-service charge can happen after turnaround. If arrival is at end of slot 24, turnaround uses slot 25? Then post-service charge can start at 26. But M04 release is 26, so we could depart at 26, but charging at slot 26 would conflict with departure? Typically, you cannot charge and depart at the same slot. So you need to have the charge completed before departure. So if we need charge, we must finish charging before departure. With depart slot 26, we could charge at slot 25 if that is after turnaround. But if turnaround occupies slot 25, then charge would be at slot 26? That would delay departure to 27. But M04 due 30, so possible. However, baseline has M04 depart 26 with 0 charge blocks. But with the closure, the only possibility for M04 to depart 26 without charge would be if M06 energy consumption is lower? No, PATH_11 energy is 1.235, so after M06 SOC would be 3.132559, and M04 needs 3.735, so must charge. So baseline is infeasible for UAV_03 due to closures and initial SOC unchanged for UAV_03? Wait, initial SOC for UAV_03 is not overridden; it's still 4.536474. So even in baseline, with M06 using PATH_09 (energy 0.154382)? In baseline, M06 had path_09, depart 10. But now COR_10 closed, so must use PATH_11. Baseline had M06 path_09, which is now closed. So UAV_03 baseline is infeasible. So we need to find new plan for UAV_03.\n\nAlso check UAV_02: initial SOC overridden to 1.781578. UAV_02: start at VP_03, available 7. M01 release 7, due 13. Path options: PATH_01 (0.240826, 2 slots), PATH_03 (4.214448, 7 slots), PATH_05 (0.226, 3 slots). With initial SOC 1.781578, reserve 1.5. Needed energy: service flight energy + reserve must be <= initial SOC (since no incoming deadhead). For M01: if we choose PATH_01: need 0.240826+1.5=1.740826, which is less than 1.781578, so feasible without charge. PATH_03: need 4.214+1.5=5.714 > 1.781578, infeasible. PATH_05: 0.226+1.5=1.726 < 1.781578, feasible. So M01 can depart at 7 with PATH_01 or PATH_05. PATH_01: 2 slots, arrival at 9. Due 13, so on time. PATH_05: 3 slots, arrival 10, also on time. Turnaround 1 slot. Then M02: release 16, due 25. Orig VP_01, dest VP_06. After M01, we have turnaround at VP_01 (slots 9-10 if PATH_01 arrival 9? Actually arrival slot is t+travel, so if depart 7, travel 2, arrival 9. Turnaround slot 9-10? Or 9-10? Then post-service charge from 10. Then we can depart M02 at slot 16 or later. SOC after M01: initial 1.781578 - energy (0.240826 for PATH_01) = 1.540752. Need for M02: energy PATH_02 0.09633 + reserve 1.5 = 1.59633. So 1.540752 is less than 1.59633, so insufficient. So we must charge after M01 to raise SOC. We can charge at VP_01 after turnaround. Max charge blocks: 3. Charge per block for UAV_02: 1.2 kWh. Capacity 16. So we can charge up to needed. So after M01, we can charge some blocks. Let's calculate: need 1.59633, have 1.540752, short by 0.055578. One charge block gives 1.2, so one block is enough. But we need to also consider later missions? Only M02 is the last mission for UAV_02. So we can do 1 charge block. But also pad capacity at VP_01? Pad capacity default 2, no override. Charger capacity at VP_01: charger_capacity_by_slot_default is 2, no override. So charging possible. So we can depart M02 at release 16 with PATH_02, travel 1, arrive 17. Due 25, ok. But we need to ensure corridor COR_05 open: no closure. So feasible.\n\nBut we must check pad capacity at VP_03 for M01 departure: pad capacity override at VP_03 slot 7 reduced to 1. So only one UAV can depart from VP_03 at slot 7. Both UAV_02 and UAV_03 start at VP_03 and are available at slot 7. M03 also departs at 7 from VP_03. So pad capacity at VP_03 slot 7 is 1, so only one can depart at 7. So one must delay departure. Which one? We need to reschedule. Also, corridor capacity? Both departures might use corridors. M01 PATH_01 uses COR_11, M03 uses COR_12. Corridor capacities are 2 for each, no overrides, so no conflict. But pad capacity forces a delay of one UAV's departure.\n\nThus, we need to choose which UAV_02 or UAV_03 departs at slot 7. The other must depart later. But we also have due windows. M03 has due 9, very tight. M01 due 13. So it's more urgent to get M03 on time. So likely we should give the pad slot 7 to M03 (UAV_03) and delay UAV_02 M01 departure to slot 8? But is pad capacity constraint per slot? Pad capacity is number of simultaneous departures/arrivals? The capacity is for each slot. So at slot 7, only one UAV can occupy a pad at VP_03. So M01 cannot depart at 7. The next slot when pad is free? Pad capacity is 1 at slot 7 only, after that default 2? The override is only for slot 7 (capacity 1). So from slot 8 onward, pad capacity at VP_03 is 2. So one could depart at 8. But release for M01 is 7, so earliest possible depart is 7, but if pad occupied, we can depart at 8. M01 due 13, so even departing at 8, travel 2 arrival 10, still due 13 ok. Or depart at 9 arrival 11, still ok. So we can delay M01.\n\nBut we must also consider the battery and charging plan for UAV_02. If we delay M01 departure to 8, then arrival at VP_01 at 10 (if PATH_01, 2 slots). Turnaround at VP_01 slot 10-11? Then we cannot start charging until 11. If we need to depart M02 at 16, we need to have charging done by then. With arrival 10, turnaround 10-11? Actually, if departure 8, travel 2, arrival at 10. Then turnaround occupies the next slot(s). The baseline has turnaround_slots=1, so turnaround occupies 1 slot after arrival. So if arrival at 10 (end of slot 10), turnaround slot is 11? This is ambiguous. Let's define: travel slots are number of slots from departure to arrival. Typically, if depart at time t, the UAV is in flight during slots t, t+1, ..., t+travel_slots-1, and arrives at the start of slot t+travel_slots? Or arrives at the end? The baseline: M01 baseline depart 7, travel 7 (PATH_03), arrival? Not given, but M02 depart 16. If arrival is at slot 7+7=14, then turnaround 1 slot (14-15), then charge? Baseline has charge_blocks=0, so no charge, so M02 depart 16. So arrival at 14, turnaround 14-15, then ready at 16. That fits. So arrival slot = departure slot + travel_slots. Then turnaround occupies the slot [arrival, arrival+?] but likely the UAV is unavailable during the turnaround slot(s). In the order, turnaround comes before post-service charge. So if arrival at 14, turnaround from 14 to 15 (1 slot), then post-service charge can start at 15? But then M02 depart 16, so a gap. So it's consistent: arrival at 14, unavailable until 15 due to turnaround, then free from 15 onwards. So if we depart at 7, travel 2, arrival 9, turnaround 9-10, free from 10. If we depart at 8, travel 2, arrival 10, turnaround 10-11, free from 11. So to depart M02 at 16, we have enough time to charge.\n\nNow, check UAV_03 plan with M03 depart at 7 (if given pad). M03 PATH_07 travel 2, arrival 9 (due 9, on time perfect). Turnaround 9-10, free from 10. Then M06 (seq1) must depart after release 10. But we have the corridor closure issue. If M03 arrives VP_01 at 9, turnaround until 10, then at VP_01. M06 origin VP_01. We can depart M06 as early as 10. But we saw PATH_06 (COR_07) closed 10-21, PATH_09 (COR_10) closed 10-47, only PATH_11 (COR_02) open, travel 14. So if depart M06 at 10 with PATH_11, arrival 24. Then turnaround at VP_07 24-25, free from 25. M04 release 26, due 30, travel 1. Need to charge to raise SOC for M04. As calculated, after M06 arrival, SOC would be 3.132559 (if no charge after M03). We can charge after M06 turnaround. If arrival 24, turnaround 24-25, post-service charge from 25. We need to charge at least 1 block to reach 3.132559+0.95=4.082559, which is >3.735059 needed. So after 1 charge block at slot 25, we are ready. But can we depart M04 at 26? The post-service charge block occupies slot 25? Typically, each charge block takes one full slot. If we start charging at 25, then slot 25 is used for charging. So the UAV is unavailable to depart at 26? Actually, if we charge at slot 25, the charge completes at the end of slot 25, so the UAV is free from slot 26. So departure at 26 is feasible. So that works. But we must check charger capacity at VP_07 for slot 25: default 2, no override, so ok. So UAV_03 feasible.\n\nBut we also need to consider that M06 release is 10, due 27, depart 10 with PATH_11 arrival 24 is fine. So UAV_03 plan: M03 depart 7 path_07 charge_blocks 0; M06 depart 10 path_11 charge_blocks 0; M04 depart 26 path_04 charge_blocks 1. But note: the charge_blocks is attached to the mission at its destination, so M04's decision includes charge_blocks that occur after M04? Actually, the schema: charge_blocks is \"The charge_blocks attached to a mission occurs at that mission's destination after its service flight and the stated turnaround. It cannot power that mission's incoming deadhead, service flight, or reserve check. Its energy is available only to later missions assigned to the same UAV.\" So we attach charge blocks to a mission to indicate the number of full charge blocks performed at its destination after it completes. For M04, it is the last mission, so no later missions. We could charge after M04, but it's not needed. In our plan, we need to charge after M06, before M04. So that charge should be attached to M06, not M04. Because it occurs after M06's turnaround at VP_07. So in decision for M06, we set charge_blocks=1 (or more if needed). Then the energy is added to UAV_03's energy before M04 starts. For M04, charge_blocks can be 0 (no need to charge after). So that works.\n\nNow, UAV_01: only MISSION_05. seq0, release 22, due 29. Orig VP_03, dest VP_04. Paths: PATH_08 (1.24 km? energy 0.147827, travel 2, max_wind 1) and PATH_10 (1.0 km, energy 0.113, travel 2, max_wind 0). No corridor closures affect these: PATH_08 uses COR_01 and COR_09, PATH_10 uses COR_03, all no closures. So feasible. UAV_01 starts at VP_03, available slot 7, but M05 release 22, so it can't depart before 22. No conflict with pad at slot 7 since it's not departing then. So UAV_01 can depart at 22. Check SOC: initial 7.5, reserve 1.5. M05 energy 0.1478 (or 0.113). So plenty. No need to charge. Decision: path options: choose one that minimizes distance? Since lexicographic includes repair distance, we'd want shorter distance. PATH_10 distance 1.0 km, energy 0.113. PATH_08 distance 1.24 km, energy 0.147827. So PATH_10 is better for distance and energy. Also PATH_10 max_wind 0, no issue. So UAV_01: M05 depart 22, path_10, charge_blocks 0.\n\nNow we must also respect the lexicographic objectives: minimize holds. Since we can schedule all missions feasibly (with M01 delayed to 8), no holds needed. So total holds = 0. Repair distance: sum of distances flown. M01: PATH_01 distance 1.92 (if we choose PATH_01) or PATH_05 distance 2.0. So PATH_01 is shorter distance 1.92, also less energy? energy 0.240826 vs 0.226? PATH_05 has slightly less energy, but distance is higher. The objective is repair distance first, then weighted tardiness, then delay plus charge wait, then energy. So we minimize distance. So between feasible paths for M01, PATH_01 distance 1.92, PATH_05 distance 2.0. So choose PATH_01. But we need to check if PATH_01 is compatible with UAV_