lay M06 to 22, we can avoid charging entirely. Distance is lower, no holds, all on time? M06 due 27, arrival 24 ok. M04 release 26, depart 26? After M06 arrival 24, turnaround 24-25, free from 25. So we can depart M04 at 26. Perfect. So that is a better plan for UAV_03: M03 depart 7; M06 depart 22 path_06 charge_blocks 0; M04 depart 26 path_04 charge_blocks 0. But we must check pad capacity at VP_01 for M06 departure at 22. VP_01 pad capacity default 2, no constraints. Corridor capacity: COR_07 at slot 22? The closure for COR_07 is slots 10-21, so 22 is open. So feasible.\n\nNow check UAV_02: we delayed M01 to 8 to avoid pad conflict, using PATH_01 arrival 10. Then we need to charge after M01 to make M02 feasible. Let's recalc with PATH_01: energy 0.240826. After M01, SOC = 1.781578 - 0.240826 = 1.540752. M02 needs 0.09633+1.5=1.59633. So need 0.055578. One charge block gives 1.2, so 1 block is sufficient. So after M01, turnaround at VP_01 from 10 to 11 (if arrival 10), then charge at 11? Can we charge at slot 11? Then post-service charge occupies slot 11. Then we are free from slot 12. M02 release 16, so we can depart at 16. So we need to set M01 charge_blocks=1. But careful: charge_blocks and timing. M01 decision has charge_blocks. That means after M01 turnaround at VP_01, we charge that many blocks. So if we set charge_blocks=1, then after M01 turnaround, we charge for 1 slot (slot 11). Then SOC becomes 1.540752 + 1.2 = 2.740752 (but capped at battery capacity 16, so no issue). Then M02 departure at 16. So perfectly feasible. So UAV_02 plan: M01 depart 8 path_01, charge_blocks=1; M02 depart 16 path_02, charge_blocks=0.\n\nBut is M01 depart 8 compatible with its release 7? Yes, release means it cannot depart before 7, but can depart after. So 8 is fine.\n\nNow check pad capacity at VP_03 for slot 8: default 2, so okay. Departure at 8 from VP_03 with PATH_01 (COR_11) is fine.\n\nNow check corridor capacity: COR_11 used by M01 at slot 8. Any other missions using COR